(a) Pressure
Pressure is defined as force divided by the area to which the force is applied. The greater the force acting on a given area, the greater the pressure. The origin of the force exerted by a gas is the incessant battering of the molecules on the walls of its container. The collisions are so numerous that they exert an effectively steady force, which is experienced as a steady pressure. The SI unit of pressure, the pascal (Pa), is defined as 1 newton per metre-squared:
1 Pa = 1 N m−2 [1.2a]
In terms of base units,
1 Pa = 1 kg m−1 s−2 [1.2b]
Several other units are still widely used (Table 1.1); of these units, the most commonly used are atmosphere (1 atm = 1.013 25 × 105 Pa exactly) and bar (1 bar = 105 Pa). A pressure of 1 bar is the standard pressure for reporting data; we denote it p7.
