(b) The features of the equation
The principal features of the van der Waals equation can be summarized as follows.
(1) Perfect gas isotherms are obtained at high temperatures and large molar volumes.
When the temperature is high, RT may be so large that the first term in eqn 1.21b greatly exceeds the second. Furthermore, if the molar volume is large in the sense Vm >> b, then the denominator Vm − b ≈ Vm. Under these conditions, the equation reduces to p = RT/Vm, the perfect gas equation.
(2) Liquids and gases coexist when cohesive and dispersing effects are in balance.
The van der Waals loops occur when both terms in eqn 1.21b have similar magnitudes. The first term arises from the kinetic energy of the molecules and their repulsive interactions; the second represents the effect of the attractive interactions.
