In thermodynamics, the total energy of a system is called its internal energy, U. The internal energy is the total kinetic and potential energy of the molecules in the system (see Comment 1.3 for the definitions of kinetic and potential energy).1 We denote by ∆U the change in internal energy when a system changes from an initial state i with internal energy Ui to a final state f of internal energy Uf :
∆U = Uf − Ui [2.1]


